  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Signal is a free, open-source, and highly secure messaging app focused on user privacy. It utilizes advanced end-to-end encryption, known as the Signal Protocol, to protect all messages, voice/video calls, and file transfers, ensuring only the intended recipient can read them. The app is available for iOS, Android, Windows, Mac, and Linux. How Signal Works End-to-end encryption: Signal secures all communication by default, meaning messages are encrypted before they leave your device and can only be decrypted by the recipient. Privacy-first: The app operates under a non-profit foundation, ensuring it does not collect user data, sell personal information, or allow for third-party snooping. Protocol: It utilizes the open-source Signal Protocol, which is considered a industry standard for secure communication and is used by other messaging services to ensure high-level security. Key Benefits of Using Signal Maximum Privacy: No data collection and no tracking. Secure Communication: Offers encrypted voice and video calls and secure, disappearing group chats. Independent: Developed by a non-profit, making it reliable for users seeking secure, non-commercial alternatives. Advanced Features: Users can now set usernames to hide their phone numbers from contacts for enhanced privacy. How to Use Signal Download and Setup: Download from the Google Play Store or the Apple App Store, then register with your phone number. Setup Desktop/Tablet: Link your mobile app to the desktop client (Windows 10/11, macOS 12+, or Linux). Messaging: Start a chat just like other messenger apps, with options for sending messages, media, and making voice or video calls. Security Settings: Set messages to disappear, verify safety numbers, and set up user names for added security.",
